\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Aggregate robust accuracy (\%) on the CIFAR-10 evaluation suite, reported as mean $\pm $ standard deviation across $n=20$ seeds. AutoAttack is reported separately and is not included in Mean(8). Bold values indicate the best method for each metric.}}{54}{table.caption.28}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Effect-size summary: AttackDRO++ (Ours) vs.\ each baseline ($n=20$). $\mitDelta $ in percentage points; negative values mean the baseline is stronger.}}{55}{table.caption.30}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Paired aggregate robustness comparison between AttackDRO++ (Ours) and each baseline. \(\mitDelta \) is computed as AttackDRO++ minus the corresponding baseline in percentage points. The \(p\)-value is from a two-sided paired \(t\)-test.}}{56}{table.caption.32}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Multiple-comparison-corrected paired significance tests for AttackDRO++ (Ours) against each baseline across $n=20$ paired seeds. $\mitDelta $ is computed as AttackDRO++ minus the corresponding baseline in percentage points. The table reports bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals, seed-level win counts, Cohen's paired effect size $d_z$, paired $t$-test $p$-values, Wilcoxon signed-rank $p$-values, and Holm-corrected Wilcoxon $p$-values. Holm correction is applied within each baseline comparison across the seven reported metrics. AutoAttack is excluded from this table and reported separately in Section~\ref {subsec:ch6_worst_case_behavior}.}}{57}{table.caption.33}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Per-attack robust accuracy (\%) for all four methods, $n=20$ seeds. Bold cells indicate the highest accuracy for each attack. The right-hand $\mitDelta $ and $p$ columns report the paired comparison of AttackDRO++ (Ours) against Multi-AT. AttackDRO++ (Ours) wins the highest score on 4 of 8 attacks; the two single-attack baselines together account for the remaining 4, each winning on the family they were trained against. $\mitDelta $ values use the bold-marker convention $^*$ for $p<0.05$ and $^{**}$ for $p<0.01$.}}{59}{table.caption.34}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces Worst(8) and 512-sample AutoAttack sanity results across $n=20$ seeds. Worst(8) is the minimum robust accuracy over the eight non-AutoAttack evaluation attacks. For AttackDRO++ $-$ Multi-AT, the paired differences are $-0.003$~pp on Worst(8) ($p=0.971$) and $-0.001$~pp on AutoAttack ($p=0.998$). Bold values indicate the highest method.}}{60}{table.caption.35}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.7}{\ignorespaces Full-test AutoAttack-$\ell _\infty $ results on the selected $n=5$ paired seed panel. The paired difference is AttackDRO++ $-$ Multi-AT: $-0.006$~pp, with $p=0.9584$.}}{60}{table.caption.36}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.8}{\ignorespaces Variance reduction across \(n=20\) training seeds. \(\mitsigma \) is the standard deviation of the per-seed metric. AttackDRO++ substantially reduces seed-to-seed variation on Mean(8), held-out averages, and held-out norm-specific metrics, while Worst(8) and AutoAttack standard deviations remain similar across methods.}}{61}{table.caption.39}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.9}{\ignorespaces Per-(class $\times $ attack) whitebox comparison of AttackDRO++ against each baseline. $\mitDelta $ is computed as AttackDRO++ minus the baseline. Positive cells count the number of attack--class cells where $\mitDelta >0$ out of 80 total cells.}}{66}{table.caption.49}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.10}{\ignorespaces Whitebox sanity check from the graybox pipeline. The \(n=5\) columns use diagonal graybox entries, where surrogate and target are the same checkpoint. The \(n=20\) columns report the main whitebox results from Table~\ref {tab:ch6_aggregate_results}.}}{70}{table.caption.54}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.11}{\ignorespaces Paired graybox comparisons. Each row uses $n=200$ paired target--surrogate--attack triples. Positive $\mitDelta $ favors the reference method named in the panel heading.}}{74}{table.caption.58}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.12}{\ignorespaces Per-attack symmetric graybox comparison between AttackDRO++ and Multi-AT. Each attack uses \(n=25\) paired seed combinations. Bold deltas are significant at \(p<0.05\).}}{75}{table.caption.59}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.13}{\ignorespaces Cross-method graybox accuracy by attack family. Values are off-diagonal target accuracy (\%) averaged over the \(n=5\) panel. Bold values mark the best method in each column.}}{76}{table.caption.60}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.14}{\ignorespaces Whitebox--graybox gap by target method on the \(n=5\) panel. The gap is cross-method graybox Mean(8) minus diagonal whitebox Mean(8).}}{76}{table.caption.61}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.15}{\ignorespaces Per-(class \(\times \) attack) graybox tests comparing AttackDRO++ against each baseline. \(\mitDelta \) is computed as AttackDRO++ minus the baseline.}}{79}{table.caption.66}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.16}{\ignorespaces AttackDRO++ (Ours) gain decomposition. Values are percentage-point changes.}}{82}{table.caption.70}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.17}{\ignorespaces Number-of-clusters ablation for AttackDRO++. The number of clusters $K$ determines the granularity of the latent adversarial groups used by Cluster-DRO. M(8) is the mean robust accuracy over the eight bounded evaluation attacks and excludes AutoAttack.}}{85}{table.caption.73}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.18}{\ignorespaces Cluster-refresh schedule ablation for AttackDRO++ with Anchor35, GradFP, and $K=4$. Values are robust accuracy (\%) reported as mean $\pm $ standard deviation over three seeds. Mean(8) averages over the eight bounded evaluation attacks and excludes AutoAttack. Worst(8) is the minimum robust accuracy over the same eight bounded attacks and also excludes AutoAttack. AutoAttack is reported separately.}}{86}{table.caption.74}%
